  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nA shortwave trough is traversing across southern IA with a cold\nfront extending southwest IA into southeastern WI. Near the \nintersection of the shortwave and front is a cluster of showers \nand embedded thunderstorms that will continue to spread into \nnorthern IL and eventually northwest IN through the evening. \nWhile lightning coverage within the cluster has been rather \nlimited, recent amdar soundings out of MDW do show some decent \ninstability present which may support an isolated lightning \nstrike or two through midnight. However, the main concern \nlocally will just be scattered showers that at times could \ncontain locally heavy downpours. Given the more scattered nature\nof the showers the threat for widespread flash flooding is low.\nThough, the combination of the slow front progression and the \nsteering flow parallel to the frontal zone may result in some \ntraining of showers which could allow a localized flash flooding\nthreat to materialize if more robust showers traverse over \nurban and/or poor drainage areas. \n\nAs the front slowly meanders across the area tonight, showers \nshould taper from north to south. However, with the frontal \nboundary forecast to stall across the southern half of the area \n(namely near or just south of Pontiac to Valparaiso line) some \nshowers will likely linger through the night and into the day on\nThursday. That said, where rain does subside the lingering low-\nlevel moisture and light winds will create favorable conditions \nfor fog development. With skies expected to remain overcast \ntonight fog coverage and intensity is uncertain, but with the \nstrong signal in guidance have opted to introduce 20-30% fog \ncoverage to the forecast for tonight mainly outside of the urban\ncore of Chicago.\n\nAside from a 20-30% chance for some lingering showers and \nisolated storms Thursday afternoon south of the Kankakee River \nValley, mostly dry conditions are expected for Thursday. Skies \nwill unfortunately remain mostly cloudy through much of the day \nwhich will only allow temperatures to warm into the upper 70s to\nnear 80. Clouds are expected to clear out from north to south \nThursday night which may set up another favorable period of \npatchy fog. With fog coverage once again being a point of lower \nconfidence have opted to forego a formal mention in the forecast\nat this time. \n\nBy Friday the bulk of the showers should be well south of our \narea leaving us with another dry and seasonably warm day with \nhighs in the mid-80s. However, another shortwave trough is \nforecast to dive across the area Friday afternoon/evening which \nmay result in a few isolated showers or storms. Forecast \nsoundings Friday afternoon and evening do show some decent \ndrying in the mid-levels which should keep a cap in place and \ntherefore hinder shower/storm coverage. For now have opted to \nkeep the 20-40% POPs offered from the NBM but suspect we will be\nable to lower these should these trends continue. \n \nHeading into the weekend, a strengthening upper ridge in the \nsouthwest CONUS will begin to amplify the northwest flow over \nthe Great Lakes. As this occurs guidance hints that several \nshortwaves should be able to pivot through the pattern and \nresult in periods of showers and thunderstorms locally. \nCurrently the two favored timeframes for stormy weather are \nSunday and again on Monday when the forecasted shortwave looks \nto be interacting with a frontal zone. Though, it should be \nnoted that things could shift as we get closer so confidence on \nexact timing and coverage of thunderstorms is low at this time. \nAs we get towards the middle of next week, the storm threat \nshould gradually shift south of the area but again a lot of this\nwill depend on how storms play out earlier in the week. Bottom \nline is that the early part of next week looks active so make \nsure to keep tabs on the forecast. Regardless, temperatures do \nlook to remain seasonable (highs in the 80s and lows in the 60s)\nthrough at least early next week.\n\nYack",
